%I A195608 #28 Nov 17 2026 15:58:24
%S A195608 1,7,11,12,13,19,20,21,25,31,35,36,37,41,47,48,49,55,59,60,61,67,68,
%T A195608 69,73,79,80,81,87,91,92,93,97,103,107,108,109,115,116,117,121,127,
%U A195608 131,132,133,137,143,144,145,151,155,156,157,161,167,171,172,173,179
%N A195608 Numbers k such that Sum_{i=1..k} A(i) = A(k)*A(k+1)/4, where A(k) = A001969(k).
%C A195608 Conjecture: There are only 3 different first differences 1,4,6.
%C A195608 Conjecture: The sequence contains either isolated series of terms, e.g., {1},{7},{25},{31},..., or series of 3 consecutive integers, e.g., {11,12,13}, {19,20,21}, etc.
%C A195608 Conjecture: The first terms m of every series satisfy the condition A(m+1)-A(m-1)=5, where A(n)=A001969(n).
%C A195608 These three conjectures are proved in Wang and Dong (2026). The third statement requires m > 1, since A001969(0) is undefined. - _Qichao Wang_, Nov 11 2026
%H A195608 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A195608/b195608.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A195608 Qichao Wang and Daoyu Dong, <a href="https://github.com/dongxuelian2/oeis-papers/blob/main/papers/A195608/main.pdf">A Binary Characterization of OEIS A195608</a> (2026).
%F A195608 Let t(k) = A010060(k). Then n belongs to this sequence if and only if either n = 2k+1 with t(k) = 0 (k >= 0), or n = 2k with t(k-1) = t(k) = 0 (k >= 1). - _Qichao Wang_, Nov 11 2026
%t A195608 a = Select[Range[0, 1000], EvenQ[DigitCount[#, 2][[1]]] &]; t = {}; s = 0; Do[s = s + a[[n]]; If[s == a[[n]] a[[n + 1]]/4, AppendTo[t, n]], {n, Length[a] - 1}]; t (* _T. D. Noe_, Sep 23 2011 *)
%Y A195608 Cf. A001969, A195467.
%K A195608 nonn,base,changed
%O A195608 1,2
%A A195608 _Vladimir Shevelev_, Sep 21 2011
